  "notes": "Indonesian is boundary-matched, so bare morphemes are safe; I still preferred domain wording (bea masuk, harga eceran tertinggi, pungutan ekspor) over literal glosses. Movement carries the meN-/di-/peN--an triad because Indonesian headlines alternate freely between active, passive and nominalised forms. Deliberately dropped 'permen' as an abbreviation of peraturan menteri (as a standalone word it means candy), 'pmk' (reads as foot-and-mouth disease in agriculture reporting), and bare 'uu' (too short and noisy)."
